💭 Hello World, assembled by a small cast of objects.
💭
💭 Emojicode's joke is not that it is hard to read — it is that it is a real
💭 language wearing a clown suit. So this greeting is deliberately
💭 over-engineered: a protocol, a value type, a class, an optional and a list,
💭 which is roughly what an enterprise Java Hello World looks like, except
💭 every type and method here is named with an emoji and every variable is not.

💭 A protocol. Anything that can hand over a piece of the greeting.
🐊 🗣 🍇
  ❗️ 💬 ➡️ 🔡
🍉

💭 A value type: a word. Copied when passed around, like an integer.
🕊 📝 🍇
  🐊 🗣

  🖍🆕 word 🔡

  🆕 🍼 word 🔡 🍇🍉

  ❗️ 💬 ➡️ 🔡 🍇
    ↩️ word
  🍉
🍉

💭 A class: punctuation, which may or may not be there. Heap-allocated,
💭 passed by reference, and holding an optional 🍬🔡 rather than a 🔡.
🐇 ✂️ 🍇
  🐊 🗣

  🖍🆕 mark 🍬🔡

  🆕 🍼 mark 🍬🔡 🍇🍉

  ❗️ 💬 ➡️ 🔡 🍇
    💭 Conditional unwrapping: the block runs only if there is a value.
    ↪️ mark ➡️ actual 🍇
      ↩️ actual
    🍉
    ↩️ 🔤🔤
  🍉
🍉

🏁 🍇
  💭 Five speakers. Two words, two marks, and one mark that isn't there.
  🍿
    🆕📝 🔤Hello🔤❗️
    🆕✂️ 🔤, 🔤❗️
    🆕📝 🔤World🔤❗️
    🆕✂️ 🔤!🔤❗️
    🆕✂️ 🤷‍♀️❗️
  🍆 ➡️ cast

  💭 Ask each one what it has to say, in order.
  🆕🍨🐚🔡🍆❗️ ➡️ 🖍🆕 words
  🔂 speaker cast 🍇
    🐻 words 💬 speaker❗️❗️
  🍉

  💭 Join with no separator, and say the whole thing exactly once.
  😀 🆕🔡 words 🔤🔤❗️❗️
🍉
